---
<Site {...meta}>
<section class="post-hero">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="crumbs"><a href="index.html">Home</a> / <a href="blog.html">Blog</a> / Dull teeth</p>
    <p class="eyebrow">Guide &middot; Spokane, Cheney &amp; Airway Heights</p>
    <h1>This is what a dull stump grinder tooth looks like.</h1>
    <p class="lede">We stopped at a local hardware store's rental yard this week and took two photos of the teeth on the stump grinder out front. If you're thinking about renting one anywhere in Spokane County, these two pictures are the whole post. The rest is what they mean and what to do about it.</p>
    <ul class="meta-row">
      <li>Spokane County, WA</li>
      <li>October 2026</li>
      <li>5 min read</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="stats">
      <div class="stat"><b>1/16 in</b><span>Tip radius where a tooth is done</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>12 hp</b><span>The small rental machine that can't bully a dull tooth</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>60 sec</b><span>How long the check takes at the counter</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container case-layout">

    <article class="article">

      <h2 id="photos">The two photos</h2>
      <p>Same machine, two teeth, taken standing in the lot. We've left them uncropped.</p>
      <div class="photo-pair">
        <figure class="post-figure">
          <Img file="rental-grinder-tooth-worn-edge.jpg" alt="Close-up of a stump grinder tooth on a rental machine: the carbide tip is rounded over and polished smooth instead of coming to an edge" widths={[480, 683]} sizes="(max-width: 48rem) 100vw, 448px" loading="eager" />
          <figcaption>The business end. Where there should be an edge, there's a polished dome.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="post-figure">
          <Img file="rental-grinder-tooth-worn-pocket.jpg" alt="Side view of a worn stump grinder tooth seated in its pocket on the cutting wheel of a rental machine, tip flattened and rounded" widths={[480, 683]} sizes="(max-width: 48rem) 100vw, 448px" />
          <figcaption>Side view in the pocket. The flat on top is wood that rubbed, not steel that cut.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <h2 id="what-you-see">What you're looking at</h2>
      <p>A stump grinder doesn't chew wood the way a chainsaw does. It carries a row of carbide-tipped teeth on a spinning wheel and swings them through the stump; each tooth takes a small bite on each pass. A fresh carbide tooth comes to a defined edge. As it wears, that edge rounds over, and the bite gets smaller every hour it runs.</p>
      <p>Look at the first photo. The tip has no edge left at all. It's a dome, polished bright from rubbing. That bright, smooth look is the tell: cutting leaves a tooth dull grey and sharp-cornered; rubbing leaves it shiny and round. The second photo shows the same thing from the side, seated in its pocket, with a flat worn into the top of the tip.</p>
      <div class="callout">
        <p><strong>Our rule of thumb:</strong> once the tip is rounded to about a 1/16-inch radius, the tooth is done. It'll still look like a tooth and it'll still spin, but it isn't cutting anymore. Both teeth in these photos are well past that.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="why-it-matters">Why it ruins a rental day</h2>
      <p>On a big commercial grinder, dull teeth cost you time. On a small rental grinder, they can cost you the whole stump. The towable units most yards rent run a 12-horsepower engine. Sharp, that engine bites and throws chips. Dull, it polishes the stump, bogs down, heats up, and turns a two-hour job into a six-hour one that may not finish. There's no power in reserve to force a rounded tooth through hardwood the way a 25-horsepower tracked machine can.</p>
      <p>We wrote the long version of this in our <a href="blog-rent-or-hire.html#teeth">rent-or-hire guide</a>. The short version: <strong>on a low-horsepower machine, sharp teeth make or break your day</strong>, and nobody at the yard is going to check them for you.</p>

      <h2 id="why-yards-miss-it">Why the rental counter doesn't catch it</h2>
      <p>This isn't a knock on the yard. A rental counter checks what a rental counter can check: the engine starts, the wheel spins, the trailer lights work. Teeth are a wear item. Most yards bill them per tooth when they come back broken, and they usually sit outside the damage waiver. Whether the set on the machine is sharp when it goes out is, in practice, your problem the moment you sign.</p>
      <p>The machine in these photos was sitting out front ready to rent. It would have started. The wheel would have spun. And somebody was going to tow it home.</p>

      <h2 id="the-check">The 60-second check before you tow it</h2>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Get down and look at the wheel.</strong> You want to see the tips of the teeth, not the side of the machine. Phone flashlight helps.</li>
        <li><strong>Look for an edge.</strong> A good tooth has a visible corner where the top meets the face. A done tooth looks like a worn pebble.</li>
        <li><strong>Look for shine.</strong> Bright, polished tips mean rubbing. Dull grey with sharp corners means cutting.</li>
        <li><strong>Check more than one.</strong> One worn tooth is normal. If most of the wheel looks like our photos, the set is done.</li>
        <li><strong>Count them.</strong> Missing teeth or empty pockets mean the wheel is out of balance and somebody's going to be billed. Make sure it's not you.</li>
        <li><strong>Say something at the counter.</strong> Ask them to swap or sharpen the set, or give you a different machine. That conversation is a lot easier in the yard than from your driveway at noon.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="local">Renting near Spokane, Cheney or Airway Heights? Check before you tow</h2>
      <p>The grinders we found for rent are in Spokane, or over in Hayden and Coeur d'Alene. For most of our customers that's a tow: out Highway 2 to <a href="community-airway-heights.html">Airway Heights</a>, down Highway 904 to <a href="community-cheney.html">Cheney</a>, up to Deer Park or Chattaroy, and back before the counter closes. A set of dull teeth discovered at home means towing it back, arguing about it, and losing the day you rented it for.</p>
      <p>That's the real cost of the photos above. Not the teeth themselves, but the hours on each side of them. If you do rent, make the check at the counter part of the pickup, the same way you'd check the trailer lights.</p>

      <h2 id="hire">Or skip the wheel entirely</h2>
      <p>Sharp teeth are our problem, not yours, when you hire it out. We bring the machine that fits the gate, we file the 811 locate as part of scheduling, and the chips either go back in the hole or leave on our trailer. Every stump gets a written estimate before anything is scheduled, and if the photo you send us shows a stump a rental machine could handle, we'll say so. We cover Spokane, Cheney, Airway Heights and the rest of the <a href="about.html#service-area">Spokane and Coeur d'Alene region</a>.</p>

      <div class="faq">
        <h2 id="faq">Common questions</h2>
        <details>
          <summary><h3>How can I tell if a stump grinder tooth is dull?</h3></summary>
          <p>Look at the carbide tip. A sharp tooth comes to a defined edge; a dull one is rounded over and polished, like a worn-down pebble. Once the tip is rounded to about a 1/16-inch radius it's done cutting, even though it still looks like a tooth and still spins.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary><h3>Do rental yards sharpen stump grinder teeth?</h3></summary>
          <p>Not routinely. Rental counters check that the machine starts and the wheel turns. Teeth are a wear item, often billed per tooth outside the damage waiver, so whether the set on the machine is sharp is yours to check before you sign.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary><h3>Can I rent a stump grinder in Cheney or Airway Heights?</h3></summary>
          <p>The grinders we found for rent are in Spokane, or over in Hayden and Coeur d'Alene, so renting from Cheney or Airway Heights means towing one out Highway 904 or Highway 2 and back the same day. Check the teeth at the counter, not in your driveway.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary><h3>What if the teeth are dull when I pick up the rental?</h3></summary>
          <p>Ask the counter to swap or sharpen them before you leave, or ask for a different machine. If they can't, a small-engine grinder with dull teeth will polish a hardwood stump instead of cutting it, and the day can end with the stump still in the ground.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

      <a class="back-link" href="blog.html">&larr; Back to all posts</a>
    </article>

